# Finder

A finder searches one defined collection and uses relevant filters to narrow its results. [Official publishing guidance](https://design-guide.publishing.service.gov.uk/frontend-templates/finder/).

**Reference pattern.** The examples document a local adaptation; they do not add a publishing backend or a new Home Assistant capability.

## When to use it

The household equivalent is a device, automation, guide or maintenance-record finder with a clearly stated scope. Start from the permitted native dataset. Explain whether the search covers entity names, friendly names, model numbers or document text. Do not combine unrelated datasets without labelling the result types.

## Structure and behaviour

Use a labelled search, useful facets, a result count and clear result links. Each result needs a stable title and enough metadata to tell it apart. Keep the current criteria when returning from a result. Provide explicit loading, no-match and unavailable states. The local finder filters three fixed example records, with readable disclosures, a matching count and a clear-filters action. It does not search Home Assistant. Without JavaScript, all records remain available and the form cannot submit.

## Home Assistant boundary

This is a reference pattern; the theme does not replace HA search or invent a registry endpoint. The child pages document every official finder variant and its local applicability. Government directory types that do not fit a household are recorded as boundaries, not implemented as fake services.

